---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const title = "Towards Sign Language-Centric Design of ASL Survey Tools";
---
<BaseLayout title={title}>
  <h1>{title}</h1>
  <p class="meta">My take on the paper.</p>

  <div class="paper">
    <p>
      This paper is a great step towards recognizing the importance of the diversity of our participants that researchers, product innovators, and many more always rely on to identify the gap in the market and research, product whitespace, and sometimes latent needs as well. For example, if we are developing a survey to identify the reason for low divorce rates in the Philippines, and if the survey language is in English, the participants might understand the language but the researchers risk misunderstanding the answer and the participants risk misunderstanding the question. This paper highlights well how there is a huge gap in research and product (websites, applications, etc) for DHH people.
    </p>

    <p>
      I appreciate the researchers' ownership of the drawbacks in the initial prototype and moving away from Qualtrics when they discovered the technology is limited. But this also highlights the problem that DHH people face on a daily basis, even a developed company does not have the functionalities to make it possible. Also, the methodology adopted to think out loud is also a great co-design methodology where the participants are not going through the iterative UI over and over again, but are explaining their thought process behind it.
    </p>

    <p>
      The prototype developed after the think out loud study is a great improvement, and I would like to suggest additional features: showing what kind of question is coming up and if there are variations of how it can be asked, showing options and the participants choosing it might increase the ease of answering. The viewer's screen could also offer a preference of viewing MCQs — options in corners versus options in a row — to accommodate individual visual attention patterns.
    </p>

    <p style="text-align: center;">
      Citation:
    </p>

    <p>
      Shruti Mahajan, Zoey Walker, Rachel Boll, Michelle Santacreu, Ally Salvino, Michael Westfort, Jeanne Reis, and Erin Solovey. 2022. Towards Sign Language-Centric Design of ASL Survey Tools. In Proc. of the 2022 CHI Conf. on Human Factors in Computing Systems (CHI '22). ACM, New York, NY, USA, Article 538, 1–16.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
